DXWEB AND DXWEB ADMIN - INSTALLATION, UPGRADES AND RECOVERY
===========================================================

Review edition: 27 October 2026
Verified manager: dxweb-manager.pl v0.49
Tested applications: DXWeb 3.0.1 and DXWeb Admin 1.0.3

1. PURPOSE AND SCOPE
-------------------

This is the sole installation manual for DXWeb and DXWeb Admin.
Both applications are part of the DXSpider Git tree at /spider.
The normal procedure uses dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl. The manager
configures systemd services and, optionally, an example Nginx
configuration. It does NOT copy or update application files:
these come exclusively from the existing Git tree.

Direct installation is the default and validated mode. It does
not require Nginx. Nginx is documented as a technical example,
without official reverse-proxy support; it has not been
revalidated in this version.

Run installation operations as root, without sudo. The account
running the manager need not be the service account. We recommend
sysop:spider where its permissions are sufficient. The manager
may retain the identity of existing units or inherit it from the
DXSpider service. To specify it explicitly, use
--user sysop --group spider.

2. FILES AND DIAGRAMS
--------------------

  /spider/INSTALL_DXWEB.es          Spanish manual
  /spider/INSTALL_DXWEB.en          British English manual
  /spider/dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl    Service manager
  /spider/dxweb/install.pl          Tree-copy installer
  /spider/dxweb/                    DXWeb application
  /spider/dxweb-admin/              DXWeb Admin application

English configuration diagram:
  /spider/dxweb/docs/DXWeb_Configuration_EN.png

Check the diagram against the ports and modes described here
before using it as a deployment reference.

The dxweb/install.pl installer has a different purpose: it
copies a DXWeb source tree to a destination and retains the
previous tree. Do not use it for a normal update of code
already integrated into /spider.

3. MODES AND PORTS
-----------------

  Mode                 DXWeb          Admin          DXSpider IntMsg
  -------------------  -------------- -------------- ---------------
  DIRECT HTTP          0.0.0.0:7380   0.0.0.0:7381   127.0.0.1:27754
  NGINX, HTTP backend  127.0.0.1:7480 127.0.0.1:7481 127.0.0.1:27754
  NGINX, public        port 7380      port 7381      not exposed

In direct mode, --scheme https configures TLS in the applications.
In Nginx mode, --scheme https configures TLS at the proxy, whilst
the applications continue to serve HTTP on loopback.

Port 27754 is not intended for browsers. Its default address is
127.0.0.1:27754. An upgrade preserves DXS_HOST and DXS_PORT
from existing service units.

4. PRE-INSTALLATION CHECKS
--------------------------

Run as root on the DXSpider server.

  cd /spider || exit 1
  ls -ld dxweb dxweb-admin
  perl -c dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl
  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --help
  id sysop
  getent group spider

If using a different service account, check that account and
group instead of sysop:spider.

Do not proceed if components or necessary permissions are missing,
or the syntax check fails. Do not discard local changes to force
an update. Check the actual service identities:

  systemctl cat dxspider.service
  systemctl cat dxweb.service
  systemctl cat dxweb-admin.service

The last two units will normally be absent on first installation.
Do not change existing service identities without first checking
access to /spider and the required operational data.

5. FIRST DIRECT HTTP INSTALLATION
---------------------------------

First inspect the plan, which does not modify services:

  cd /spider || exit 1
  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action plan \
    --root /spider --components both --mode direct \
    --scheme http --user sysop --group spider

If the plan is correct, install:

  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action install \
    --root /spider --components both --mode direct \
    --scheme http --user sysop --group spider --apply

The manager creates or updates systemd units. There is no need
to copy .service files manually from the repository.

6. DIRECT UPGRADES
-----------------

DXWeb and DXWeb Admin belong to the DXSpider repository. Their
files are updated alongside the rest of the project through
the administrator's usual DXSpider update procedure.

There is no need to download or update these components separately.

After updating DXSpider, if DXWeb or DXWeb Admin has changed,
run the installation manager using the procedure in section 5.

Inspect the plan first and, if correct, apply the changes.

The manager updates service configuration where necessary and
restarts the services to use the updated code.

DXSpider itself need not be restarted solely to apply an update
to DXWeb or DXWeb Admin.

7. INSTALLATION AND UPGRADES WITH NGINX
--------------------------------------

NOT AN OFFICIALLY SUPPORTED EXAMPLE. Manager v0.49 can generate
an Nginx configuration on the same host as DXWeb and DXWeb Admin.
A proxy on another server and subpath deployment have not been
validated here.

Check that Nginx is installed, inspect its configuration and
back it up:

  cd /spider || exit 1
  nginx -t
  ls -la /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
  mkdir -p /root/dxweb-nginx-backup
  cp -a /etc/nginx/nginx.conf /root/dxweb-nginx-backup/
  cp -a /etc/nginx/sites-available /root/dxweb-nginx-backup/
  cp -a /etc/nginx/sites-enabled /root/dxweb-nginx-backup/

Do not delete or overwrite unrelated sites. If
/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/dxweb.conf already exists, the manager
rejects the Nginx installation and requires manual migration.
Do not work around this by deleting the existing configuration.

For HTTP, replace web.example.net with the actual DNS hostname:

  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action plan \
    --root /spider --components both --mode nginx \
    --scheme http --hostname web.example.net \
    --user sysop --group spider

  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action install \
    --root /spider --components both --mode nginx \
    --scheme http --hostname web.example.net \
    --user sysop --group spider --apply

HTTPS requires a real certificate and private key that match
one another and cover the hostname. The manager checks these:

  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action plan \
    --root /spider --components both --mode nginx \
    --scheme https --hostname web.example.net \
    --cert /etc/ssl/dxweb/fullchain.pem \
    --key /etc/ssl/dxweb/privkey.pem \
    --user sysop --group spider

  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action install \
    --root /spider --components both --mode nginx \
    --scheme https --hostname web.example.net \
    --cert /etc/ssl/dxweb/fullchain.pem \
    --key /etc/ssl/dxweb/privkey.pem \
    --user sysop --group spider --apply

The certificate paths are examples; do not use them literally.
The manager does not issue certificates. The generated Nginx
configuration is stored at
/etc/nginx/sites-available/dxweb-installer.conf, with a link at
/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/dxweb-installer.conf.

The manager generates two server blocks on ports 7380 and 7381,
proxying to 127.0.0.1:7480 and 127.0.0.1:7481. It configures
WebSocket Upgrade and proxy headers. Keep internal ports
inaccessible from outside. Do not trust client-supplied headers.

MANUAL CONFIGURATION OF AN HTTP REVERSE PROXY

When configuring DXWeb or DXWeb Admin manually behind an HTTP
reverse proxy, set MOJO_REVERSE_PROXY=1 in the relevant service.
The proxy must forward the client's real IP address using
X-Real-IP or X-Forwarded-For.

Accept these headers only from trusted proxies to prevent
clients from forging their IP addresses. Check that DXWeb
correctly identifies each user's real IP, as an incorrect
address may cause other nodes to reject spots.

The manager automatically configures MOJO_REVERSE_PROXY=1 and
the necessary headers when --mode nginx is selected. These
manual instructions apply to other proxies or installations
performed without the manager.

When upgrading an Nginx installation, retain the hostname,
scheme, certificate and key. Inspect the plan before applying it.
Do not mix a direct service with the Nginx proxy without migration.

8. INSTALLING JUST ONE APPLICATION
----------------------------------

In direct mode, use --components dxweb or --components admin
instead of both. Keep the other arguments the same for planning
and installation. In Nginx mode, the manager can preserve the
other component's server block when it finds a configuration
managed by this installer in the exact format it recognises.
For a first Nginx installation, --components both is recommended.

9. CHECKING THE RESULT
----------------------

  cd /spider || exit 1
  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action status --components both
  systemctl is-active dxweb.service dxweb-admin.service
  systemctl show dxweb.service -p ActiveState -p MainPID
  systemctl show dxweb-admin.service -p ActiveState -p MainPID
  ss -lntp | grep -E ':(7380|7381|7480|7481)\b'

For a direct HTTP installation:

  curl -f http://127.0.0.1:7380/software-version.json
  curl -f http://127.0.0.1:7381/software-versions.json

In Nginx mode, check nginx -t and use the real public URL with
its http or https scheme and ports 7380 and 7381.
In a browser, check spots, SYSOP authentication, WebSockets,
supervision, filters and administrative permissions.
A successful HTTP response does not replace these checks.

10. BACKUPS AND ROLLBACK
-----------------------

The manager stores transactions involving configuration changes in:

  /var/backups/dxweb-installer/

Retain the exact transaction name. Before reverting, inspect its
manifest.json and the rollback plan. For example:

  cd /spider || exit 1
  ls -lt /var/backups/dxweb-installer/
  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action rollback \
    --backup /var/backups/dxweb-installer/TRANSACTION

Only after inspecting the result, run:

  perl dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl --action rollback \
    --backup /var/backups/dxweb-installer/TRANSACTION --apply

Rollback restores configuration and service state; it does NOT
change the Git code revision. If the transaction represents an
initial installation, the correct outcome may be removal of the
systemd units: LoadState=not-found and ActiveState=inactive.
The manager uses a bounded stop and a safety checkpoint during
recovery. Do not repeat rollback or restore units manually
without first inspecting the resulting state and logs.

Do not delete databases, filters, certificates or local
configuration as an assumed recovery procedure.

11. LOGS AND TROUBLESHOOTING
----------------------------

  journalctl -u dxweb.service -n 100 --no-pager
  journalctl -u dxweb-admin.service -n 100 --no-pager
  systemctl status dxweb.service --no-pager
  systemctl status dxweb-admin.service --no-pager

With Nginx, also check:

  nginx -t
  journalctl -u nginx.service -n 100 --no-pager

If the manager detects an incompatible Nginx site, do not
delete the existing configuration. Prepare a migration with a
prior backup. If it reports NO CONFIG CHANGES, install --apply
may still be required after updating the Git code.

12. TECHNICAL SYSTEMD EXAMPLES
------------------------------

Four independent examples may be distributed:

  dxweb.service.example
  dxweb-admin.service.example
  dxweb.service.nginx.example
  dxweb-admin.service.nginx.example

They are references for manual testing, NOT installation steps.
The manager generates installed units directly and preserves
certain pre-existing local options. The examples must therefore
represent a fresh installation with sysop:spider, /spider as
the actual path, and DXS_HOST=127.0.0.1, DXS_PORT=27754. If
/spider is a symbolic link, the manager uses the resolved
physical path; adjust the examples before installing manually.

Verify examples against the unit generator before adding them
to the repository. Do not replace a working unit with an example
without a backup and a comparison of their differences.

13. LIMITATIONS AND SECURITY
----------------------------

HTTPS is recommended whenever DXWeb or DXWeb Admin is accessible
from the Internet or other public networks.

This is particularly important for DXWeb Admin, which provides
administrative functions and requires authentication.

Reserve unencrypted HTTP for trusted local networks or
controlled test environments.

Nginx support is provided as a configuration example, without
a guarantee of compatibility in every deployment. External
proxies, subpaths and certificates require separate validation.

Do not recursively change ownership or permissions under
/spider to resolve access problems.

Installation does not require stopping or reinstalling DXSpider.
Do not use this manual to administer other projects on the node.
